{
 "format": "doorprints-kind/1",
 "id": "house",
 "version": 1,
 "note": "The kind file for houses (docs/03 ADR-36 and §18; S4b-BL-205). It describes today's house exactly: the same fields, sections, statuses, checklist and question scopes, with labels as keys that resolve to today's strings (web: the dictionaries in web/src/app/i18n, Android: android/ui/src/commonMain/composeResources). The apps read copies of it: the label resolver of kinds PR 3 (S4b-BL-207) and the typed-extras renderer of PR 4 (S4b-BL-208), whose `forCadences` hides the rent-only cost fields for a sale. `was` names where a value lives in the backup family before the format reset (houses, doorprints-backup/1 to /3); since the reset (S4b-BL-206) it is `money` or `extras.<id>` of a candidate, and `was` stays because every reader still reads the family before. A price without a type is money with an amount and no cadence (owner, 2026-10-12): never a monthly rent. kind-vectors.json pins what this file resolves to. The `ai` slots name what the house's hand-written AI code reads (S4b-BL-271; the code does not read them: a house's documents, prompts and Plan lines are pinned byte for byte and stay as they are): `extract` is what the listing extract and the no-AI parser fill (`amenities` is a hint on the draft, never stored; `areaSqft` is read by the no-AI parsers), `ask` is every line of an Ask document in its order, `plan` is what a Plan candidate shows (`street` stands in for a missing locality). house-ai-slot-vectors.json maps each line to the slot ids it carries and the three stacks' tests hold their code to it.",
